{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.95,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R34"],
      "pins": ["R34.1", "R34.2"],
      "nets": ["/CAN0_N", "Net-(R34-Pad2)"],
      "observed": "R34 is specified as 120k in the netlist, while its MPN field is 0603WAF1200T5E (120Ω) and the matching CAN1 termination resistor R35 is 120R.",
      "expected": "R34 should be 120Ω to provide standard CAN bus termination on CAN0, matching R35 on CAN1.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "R34 | 120k | Resistor_SMD:R_0603_1608Metric | MotorController:TAYLOR_RESISTOR |  | LCSC=C22787, MPN=0603WAF1200T5E, Manufacturer=UNI-ROYAL(Uniroyal Elec)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "R35 | 120R | Resistor_SMD:R_0603_1608Metric | MotorController:TAYLOR_RESISTOR |  | LCSC=C22787, MPN=0603WAF1200T5E, Manufacturer=UNI-ROYAL(Uniroyal Elec)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "/CAN0_N : ... R34.1(passive) ..."
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If assembled to the 120k value, CAN0 termination is effectively absent, causing reflections and unreliable CAN communication; if assembled to the MPN, the value field is misleading.",
      "confidence": 0.99
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U10", "U11"],
      "pins": ["U10.4", "U10.5", "U11.4", "U11.5"],
      "nets": ["GND"],
      "observed": "U10 pin 4 (GND1) and pin 5 (GND2) are both on net GND; U11 pin 4 (GND1) and pin 5 (GND2) are also both on net GND.",
      "expected": "For isolated CAN transceivers, GND1 (logic side) and GND2 (isolated bus side) must be on separate nets to maintain galvanic isolation.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "GND [High Power] : ... U10.4(GND1,power_in) U10.5(GND2,power_in) ... U11.4(GND1,power_in) U11.5(GND2,power_in) ..."
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9) through an isolated CAN transceiver (U11/companion)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The isolation barrier is bypassed, so ground loops and common-mode noise can couple between the CM4 and the CAN bus, defeating the intended isolation and potentially causing communication errors or damage.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["D30", "R60"],
      "pins": ["D30.4", "D30.2", "R60.1", "R60.2"],
      "nets": ["ESTOP_OUT", "Net-(D30-K2)"],
      "observed": "D30 pin 4 (A2) is on ESTOP_OUT, and R60 pin 2 is also on ESTOP_OUT; R60 pin 1 connects to D30 pin 2 (K2). Thus the series combination of LED2 and R60 is connected between ESTOP_OUT and ESTOP_OUT.",
      "expected": "The ESTOP_OUT indicator LED should be connected between ESTOP_OUT and GND (e.g., R60.2 to GND) so it can light when ESTOP_OUT is high.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "ESTOP_OUT : D30.4(A2,input) ... R60.2(passive) ..."
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "Net-(D30-K2) : D30.2(K2,input) R60.1(passive)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The ESTOP_OUT indicator LED never lights, so the board loses visual indication of the e-stop output state.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files contain three anomalies: R34 is incorrectly valued at 120k instead of 120Ω for CAN0 termination, the isolated CAN transceivers U10/U11 have their isolated and logic grounds tied together, and the ESTOP_OUT indicator LED D30 is wired in a loop so it can never light."
}

